# A gating ultramicroporous metal-organic framework showing high adsorption selectivity, capacity and rate for xylene separation

1Key Laboratory of Bioinorganic and Synthetic Chemistry (Ministry of Education), School of Chemistry, Sun Yat-Sen University, Guangzhou 510275,China; 2Key Lab of Fluorine and Silicon for Energy Materials and Chemistry of Ministry of Education/National Engineering Research Center for Carbohydrate Synthesis, Jiangxi Normal University, Nanchang 330022, China; 3Guangdong Provincial Key Laboratory of New and Renewable Energy Research and Development, Guangzhou Institute of Energy Conversion, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Guangzhou 510640, China
Adsorptive separation of $p$ -xylene $(p\mathrm{X})$ from xylene isomers is a key process in chemical industry, but known adsorbents cannot simultaneously achieve high adsorption selectivity, capacity, and rate. Here, we demonstrate gating ultramicropore as a solution for this challenge. Slight modification of the synthetic condition gives rise to isomeric metal-organic frameworks $\mathsf{a{-}[Z n(p b a)]}$ (MAF-88, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathsf{p b a}=4$ -( $1H.$ -pyrazol-4-yl)benzoic acid) and $\upbeta$ $\left.3{\cdot}[Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{pba})]\right.$ (MAF-89) possessing similar pillared-column structures, porosities, and high $p\mathrm{X}$ capacities of $2.0\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , but very different framework/pore topologies, pore sizes, and $p\mathrm{X}$ selectivities. For binary and ternary mixtures of liquid xylene isomers, MAF-88 with narrow one-dimensional (1D) channels shows $p\mathrm{X}$ selectivity of 11 and 1.6, while MAF-89 with 3D-connected quasi-discrete pores shows $p\mathrm{X}$ selectivity up to 221 and 46, respectively. Thermogravimetry, differential scanning calorimetry, and time-dependent separation experiments reveal that the kinetic effects of the gating pores play more important roles than the thermodynamic effects, which is further confirmed by single-crystal X-ray diffraction and computational simulations.
## metal-organic frameworks, gating, xylene, adsorption, separation
Citation: Ye ZM, Zhang XF, Liu DX, Xu YT, Wang C, Zheng K, Zhou DD, He CT, Zhang JP. A gating ultramicroporous metal-organic framework showing high adsorption selectivity, capacity and rate for xylene separation. SciChinaChem, 2022, 65: 1552–1558, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-022-1304-1
## 1 Introduction
The separation of xylene isomers, including $p$ -xylene $(p\mathrm{X})$ , $m$ -xylene $(m\mathbf{X})$ , and $o$ -xylene $(o\mathrm{X})$ , is one of the most important and challenging tasks in industry [1]. Adsorptive separation of $p\mathrm{X}$ based on zeolites is the dominant method, but the efficiency is still limited by the relatively low adsorption selectivity, capacity, and rate [2,3]. For example, the MFI-type zeolite H-ZSM-5 showed $p\mathrm{X}$ selectivities of ca. 20 and capacity of ca. $1.3~\mathrm{mmol~g}^{-1}$ [2–4], the FAU-type zeolite BaX showed $p\mathrm{X}$ selectivity of ca. 7 and capacity of ca. $1.0\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and they need over $100\mathrm{{min}}$ to reach $p\mathrm{X}$ adsorption equilibrium at ambient temperatures [4,5].
Molecule-based crystalline adsorbents, such as porous coordination polymers (PCPs), metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), and porous molecular crystals (PMCs), have shown great potentials for adsorptive separation [6–12]. Their tailorable and adaptive pore sizes/shapes can fit the target molecules to enhance the adsorption selectivity and capacity [13–18]. However, there is always a trade-off between the xylene adsorption selectivity and capacity [19]. For example, [V(OH)(bdc)] (MIL-47, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}=$ benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid) achieved a high $p\mathrm{X}$ uptake of $4.55\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , but its $p\mathrm{X}$ selectivity was only 1.1 [20]. $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}L_{2}C l_{4}]}$ $\mathrm{T}=1{,}3$ -bis-(imidazol-1-ylmethyl)-2,4,6-trimethylbenzene) and $[\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{pypz})_{2}]$ (MAF-36, $\mathrm{\sfHpypz}=4$ -( $1H.$ -pyrazol-4-yl)pyridine) showed $p\mathrm{X}$ selectivity over 50 but their $p\mathrm{X}$ uptakes are lower than $1.3\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ [21,22].
While many thermodynamic, kinetic, and even molecularsieving separation mechanisms have been reported [23–26], only ultramicropores with sizes similar to that of $p\mathrm{X}$ achieved high $p\mathrm{X}$ selectivity over 10 [2,3,21,22,27–34]. The size/shape-matched pores can achieve better guest recognition, while the ultra-small pore apertures enlarge the difference of guest adsorption/diffusion rates through the gating mechanism (transisent opening/closing of relatively small pore apertures), although these structural responses are often ignored due to the difficulty of characterization [22,23,35– 39]. However, such adsorbents generally possess discrete (0D) or one-dimensional (1D) pores with low porosities (void ratio $<20\%$ ) and high gating barriers (relatively rigid), giving low adsorption capacities $(<2.0\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and adsorption rates (need at least hours to reach equilibrium) [21,22,27–33]. In this work, we show that alteration of the ligand arrangement in the classic MOFs can effectively change the connectivity of the pore system, giving not only gating narrow pores for high $p\mathrm{X}$ selectivity but also relatively large porosity for high $p\mathrm{X}$ capacity, as well as very fast adsorption kinetics.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Synthesis
## 2.1.1 Synthesis of MAF-89·Guest
$\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ( $\mathrm{60~mg}.$ 0.2 mmol) and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{pba}$ (4-(1Hpyrazol-4-yl)benzoic acid, $37.6\mathrm{mg}$ , $0.2\:\mathrm{mmol}\dot{}.$ ) were dissolved in a mixed solvent of dimethylacetamide (DMA, $2{\mathrm{~mL~}}$ ), methanol (MeOH, 4 mL) and water $\mathrm{(H_{2}O,~1~m L)}$ ). Then the mixture was sealed in a $15~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon reactor and heated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 days. After cooling to room temperature at a rate of $10\ {^\circ}\mathrm{C/h}$ , very small needle crystals were filtered, washed by MeOH, and dried in air (yield $\sim70\%$ based on $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{p}\mathrm{b}\mathrm{a})$ . The crystals of MAF-89·Guest (ca. $100\mathrm{mg},$ ) were heated at $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ under nitrogen flow to give crystals of MAF-89. EA calculated for $[Z\mathbf{n}$ $\left(\mathbf{C}_{10}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}\right)]$ $(\mathrm{C_{10}H_{6}N_{2}O_{2}Z n})_{,}$ : C, 47.75; H, 2.40; N, 11.14. Found $(\%)$ : C, 47.19; H, 2.83; N, 10.93.
## 2.1.2 Synthesis of MAF-88·Guest
The synthesis procedure was the same for MAF-89·Guest except by changing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to toluene (yield $\sim70\%$ based on $\mathrm{H_{2}p b a})$ . The crystals of MAF-88·Guest were heated at
$250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $180\mathrm{min}$ under nitrogen flow or vacuum to give MAF-88. EA calculated for $\mathrm{[Zn(C_{10}H_{6}N_{2}O_{2})]\cdot0.5H_{2}O}$ $(\mathrm{C_{10}H_{7}N_{2}O_{2.5}Z n})$ : C, 46.10; H, 2.71; N, 10.75. Found $(\%)$ : C, 46.67; H, 2.83; N, 10.70.
## 2.2 Crystal structure determination
The unit-cell parameters of microcrystaline MAF-89 were obtained by the Pawley refinement of the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern at $2\theta=5^{\circ}-40^{\circ}$ . Peak profiles, zero-shift, background, and unit-cell parameters were refined simultaneously. The peak profiles were refined by the Pseudo-Voigt function with asymmetry correction parameters. The background was refined with a 20th-order polynomial. All operations were performed using Materials Studio 5.5. The details of the crystallographic data are summarized in Table S1 (Supporting Information online).
## 2.3 Mixture adsorption measurements
The adsorbent sample (ca. $20~\mathrm{mg}$ ) was put in a glass tube
0 $\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\textrm~{~~}~}}_{15\ensuremath{\mathrm{\mL}})}$ and heated at $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum for $12\mathrm{h}$ . After cooling down to near $308\mathrm{K}$ , the tube was refilled with nitrogen. Then equimolar $p\mathrm{X}/m\mathrm{X},p\mathrm{X}/o\mathrm{X}$ , or $\cdot p\mathrm{X}/m\mathrm{X}/o\mathrm{X}$ liquid mixture (ca. $10~\mathrm{mL}$ ) was immediately injected into the glass tube and kept in an oven at $308\mathrm{K}$ . After a certain time of interest, the adsorbent sample was filtered and then purged by $\Nu_{2}$ flow $(20\mathrm{mL}\mathrm{min}^{-1})$ at room temperature for $30\mathrm{min}$ . The total adsorption amounts were calculated by the weight losses of TG curves below $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The adsorption ratios of xylene isomers were determined by gas chromatographymass spectrometry (GC-MS) analysis. The adsorbent sample was immersed in MeOH $\mathrm{10~mL)}$ at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ and then washed with methanol (MeOH, 3 mL) for three times. The MeOH extractions were combined ( $\mathrm{19~mL}$ for GC-MS analysis. The complete extraction of the adsorbed xylene isomers was confirmed by the following procedures. The adsorbent sample after MeOH extraction was digested using nitric acid $(0.5\mathrm{mL},8\mathrm{M}$ ) followed by $n$ -heptane extraction (1 mL) for three times. The $n$ -heptane extractions were combined $(3~\mathrm{mL})$ for GC-MS analysis, which showed no peaks of xylene isomers.
## 2.4 Gas chromatography-mass spectrometry analyses
$$
S_{\mathrm{ads}}={\frac{Q_{2}/(Q_{1}+\cdots+Q_{i}-Q_{2})}{\theta_{2}/(\theta_{1}+\cdots+\theta_{i}-\theta_{2})}}
$$
where $Q_{i}$ and $\theta_{i}$ are the uptake and the molar fraction of guest $i$ remained in the liquid adsorbate, respectively, in which $\theta_{1}+$ $\dots+\theta_{i}=100\%$ . Since largely excess equimolar mixtures were used, $\theta_{i}$ is regarded as constant during the whole adsorption/separation experiments.
## 2.5 Computational simulations
All computational calculations were performed by using the Materials Studio 5.5 package. Density functional theory including dispersion correction (DFT-D) simulations were performed by the Dmol module, using the generalized gradient approximation (GGA) with the Perdew-BurkeErnzerhof (PBE) functional, Grimme as dispersion correction method and the double numerical plus d-functions (DNP) basis set. Molecular dynamics (MD) simulations adopted the isothermal-isobaric ensemble with constant pressure/temperature (NPT) using Nose thermostat and random initial velocities. The van der Waals and electrostatic interactions were evaluated by the Ewald summation method, while all the Buffer widths were set as $0.5\textup{\AA}$ . The time step was 1.0 fs and total simulation time was 200 ps under $298\mathrm{K}$ . QEq and ESP charges were employed to the host structure and guest molecules, respectivley.
## 3 Results and discussion
Solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{pba}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ in mixed $\mathrm{DMA/MeOH/H_{2}O}$ solvent yielded $\beta{\mathrm{-}}[Z{\mathrm{n}}({\mathrm{pba}})]$ Guest (MAF-89) (Figure 1 and Figure S1). Single-crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction (SCXRD) showed that MAF-89 possesses a tetragonal pillared-column structure based on 4-connected metal ions and ligands, just like $\left[Z\mathrm{n(mpba)}\right]$ (MAF-X8, $\mathrm{{H}_{2}\mathrm{{mpba}=4}}$ -(3,5-dimethylpyrazol-4-yl)benzoic acid) [40], [Co(bdpb)] (MFU-2, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdpb}=1{,}4$ -bis[(3,5-dimethyl)pyrazol-4-yl]) [41], and [M(bdp)] $\mathbf{M}=Z\mathbf{n}$ or Co, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{b}\mathrm{d}\mathsf{p}=1,4-$ bis( $1H.$ -pyrazol-4-yl)benzene) [42], in which the ligands serve as pillars and the $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ -pyrazolate/carboxylate chains serve as columns. However, the pillaring mode and pore structure are quite different between MAF-X8/MFU-2/ [M(bdp)] and MAF-89. In the former structures, the pillars are evenly arranged and perpendicular $(90^{\circ})$ to the columns, resulting in large 1D channels with sizes of ca. $9\textup{\AA}\times9\textup{\AA}$ and void ratios of ca. $50\%$ . In contrast, the pillars exhibit inclined angles of $\pm58^{\circ}$ in MAF-89, which not only change the network connectivity, but also significantly narrow the 1D channels $(4.2\mathrm{~\AA~}\times4.2\mathrm{~\AA~})$ and generate considerable voids $(5.5\mathrm{\AA}\times7.3\mathrm{\AA}\times4.5\mathrm{\AA})$ between the pillars to give a 3D pore system with a void ratio of $33.1\%$ (Figure 1a, b). The whole 3D pore system of MAF-89 can also be described as a 3- connected srs network consisting of large quasi-0D pores $(5.5\mathrm{\AA}\times7.3\mathrm{\AA}\times9.9\mathrm{\AA})$ and small apertures $(4.2\mathrm{~\AA~}\times4.2\mathrm{~\AA~})$ (Figure 1c). For comparison, the pore structure of ZSM-5 is a 4-connected dia network consisting of intersecting zigzag 1 $(5.1\mathrm{~\AA~}\times5.5\mathrm{~\AA)}$ and straight $(5.4\mathrm{~\AA~}\times5.6\mathrm{~\AA~})$ 1D channels (Figure S2) [43].
MAF-89 is a supramolecular isomer of MAF-88 with a rhombic pillared-column structure and larger yet narrow 1D channels (cross sectional size: $5.3\mathrm{~\AA~{~}}\times5.4\mathrm{~\AA~{~}}-6.4\mathrm{~\AA~{~}}\times9.6\mathrm{~\AA~{~}}$ ， void ratio: $34.7\%$ , Figure S3), which shows huge guestamplified thermal expansion [44]. Considering the metal ions and ligands both as 4-connected nodes, MAF-X8/MFU$2/[\mathrm{M}(\mathrm{bdp})]$ , MAF-88, and MAF-89 can be simplified as the pts (point symbol $4^{2}.8^{4})$ , umc (point symbol $4^{3}.{\dot{6}}^{2}.{\dot{\ell}}$ ), and the $(4^{2}.8^{\bar{4}})(4^{2}.8^{\bar{3}}.10)$ topologies [45], respectively (Figure S4). Considering the pillars and columns as linkers and their intersecting points as 4-connected nodes, MAF-X8/MFU-2/ [M(bdp)], MAF-88, and MAF-89 can be simplified as the classic cds (point symbol $6^{5}$ .8), pts (point symbol $4^{2}.8^{4})$ , and a new uninodal 4-connected topology (point symbol $6^{3}.8^{2}.10)$ , respectively (Figure 1d and Figure S5).
MAF-89 is synthesized in almost the same method as for MAF-88, except that one of the three solvents is changed from toluene to water, highlighting the structural diversity and sensitivity of this metal-ligand system [46]. PXRD showed that microcrystalline MAF-89 synthesized in the $100\mathrm{mg}$ scale is pure, confirming the synthetic controllability of this supramolecular isomer (Figure S6). Scanning electron microscope (SEM) showed a rod shape morphology with length of ca. $40\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and a diameter of ca. $3\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Figure S7).
Thermogravimetry of MAF-89 revealed a weight loss of $23.9\%$ from 50 to $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , corresponding to the complete guest removal, followed by a plateau up to the decomposition temperature of $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S8). Variable-temperature PXRD showed that the host framework, which deformed a little from the as-synthesized state, can remain intact at least to $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S9). The $77\mathrm{~K~N}_{2}$ and $195\mathrm{K}\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption isotherms both showed type-I characteristics with saturated uptakes of 6.50 and $5.52\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , corresponding to pore volumes of 0.226 and $0.220\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Figure S10), being consistent well with the value of $0.2\bar{4}1\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ calculated from the crystal structure (Table S1). The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) and Langmuir surface areas calculated from the $\Nu_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ isotherms were 562/622 and $637/723\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Figure S11).
Considering that the size/shape of the quasi-0D pore is similar with that of $p\mathrm{X}$ , and the aperture connecting the pores is quite small, MAF-89 may be suitable for the separation of $p\mathrm{X}$ from xylene isomers. The xylene separation performance of MAF-89 was investigated by batch adsorption experiment at $308\mathrm{K}$ (Figure 2). The MOF sample was immersed in excessive pure $p\mathrm{X}$ , mX, or $o X$ , or equimolar $p\mathrm{X}/m\mathrm{X}/o\mathrm{X},p\mathrm{X}/$ $m X$ or $p\mathrm{X}/o\mathrm{X}$ mixture for different time, then filtered, and dried under ${\bf N}_{2}$ flow. The total adsorption amounts were measured by TG (Figure S12), and the $p\mathrm{X}$ selectivities were analyzed through GC-MS (Figure 2a, b and Figure S13). The adsorption equilibrium of single-component $p\mathrm{X}$ , $m X$ and $\ o\mathrm{X}$ was achieved in ca. 20, 180 and $300\mathrm{min}$ , respectively (Figure 2c and Figure S14). The single-component adsorption kinetic data of $p\mathrm{X}$ , $m X$ , and $\ o\mathrm{X}$ can be well fitted by the pseudo-second-order model, which illustrates that the guest diffusion is mainly determined by the liquid/solid interface, meaning that $p\mathrm{X}$ diffusion is fast inside the crystals/pores [47]. Based on the obtained rate constants of $8.2\times{{10}^{-1}}$ , $9.3\times$ ${{10}^{-2}}$ , and $4.4\times10^{-2}\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mmol}^{-1}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ for $p\mathrm{X}$ , $m X$ , and $o X$ , respectively, the kinetic selectivity was calculated as $k_{p\mathrm{X}}/k_{m\mathrm{X}}/$ $k_{o X}=18.6/2.1/1$ (Figure S14 and Table S2). The adsorption of the xylene mixtures achieved over $90\%$ capacity of the equilibrium amount within $15\mathrm{min}$ , which was similar with [39]that of single-component $p\mathrm{X}$ (Figure 2c and Figure S12), indicating high kinetic selectivity for $p\mathrm{X}$ under mixture conditions. It is worth to note that the $p\mathrm{X}$ adsorption equilibrium time of MAF-89 (near room temperature) is one of the shortest among MOFs, zeolites and activated carbons [4,48], just longer than those of a handful of MOFs and PMCs [20,21], such as MAF-36 with small particle size of $0.5\upmu\mathrm{m}\times0.5\upmu\mathrm{m}\times1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ [22].
The adsorption equilibrium amounts in all three mixtures $(1.80{-}1.83\ \mathrm{mmol\g}^{-1})$ were almost identical, which were higher than that for single-component $m X$ and $\ o\mathrm{X}$ (1.75 and $1.50\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ but lower than that of single-component $p\mathrm{X}$ $(2.00\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , indicating highly preferential $p\mathrm{X}$ adsorption in the mixtures (Figure 2c and Figure S12). After adsorption equilibrium, the $p\mathrm{X}$ selectivities were determined as 19, 221, and 46 (Figure 2a), meaning that $p\mathrm{X}$ with purity of $95.0\%$ , $99.5\%$ , and $95.5\%$ could be produced after one adsorption-desorption cycle from equimolar $p\mathrm{X}/m\mathrm{X}$ , $p\mathrm{X}/o\mathrm{X}$ , and $p X/m X/o X$ mixtures, respectively. Note that the $p\mathrm{X}/o\mathrm{X}$ adsorption selectivity of MAF-89 is a new record [21,22,27– 31], and the $p{\mathrm{X/}}m{\mathrm{X}}$ and $p X/m X/o X$ adsorption selectivities are also among the highest (Table S3) [21,22,32,33]. Meanwhile, the $p\mathrm{X}$ selectivity for the three mixtures showed no obvious change from 1 to $720\mathrm{min}$ (Figure 2b and Figure S15), indicating negligible guest exchange like other ultramicroporous adsorbents dominated by kinetic separation mechanisms [17,30,32]. Furthermore, after 10 consecutive adsorption-desorption cycles for the $p X/m X/o X$ mixture, the total adsorption amount and $p\mathrm{X}$ selectivity changed little and the PXRD pattern remained unchanged (Figure S16), indicating high stability and recyclability of MAF-89.
For comparison, we also measured the xylene separation performances for the isomer MAF-88. The preliminary tests showed a $p\mathrm{X}$ adsorption capacity of $2.0\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is the same with that of MAF-89 (Table S4, Figures S8 and S12), being consistent with their similar porosities. However, MAF-88 exhibits moderate or low $p\mathrm{X}$ selectivities of 6.7, 10.7, and 1.6 for equimolar $p\mathrm{X}/m\mathrm{X}$ , $p\mathrm{X}/o\mathrm{X}$ , and $p X/m X/o X$ mixtures, respectively (Figure S13). The very different $p\mathrm{X}$ selectivity between MAF-88 and MAF-89 highlights the critical role of pore size especially at the apertures. High $p\mathrm{X}$ selectivity of $81.2/98.9\$ and promissing $p\mathrm{X}$ capacitiy of $2.2/2.7\ \mathrm{mmol\g}^{-1}$ were recently reported for SOD- $[Z\mathrm{n-}$ $(\mathrm{mim})_{2}]$ (MAF-4/ZIF-8, $\mathrm{Hmim}=2\cdot$ -methylimidazole) and SOD- $[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{mim})_{2}]$ (MAF-4-Co/ZIF-67) possesing 3D gating pores, but they need more than $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ to reach equilibrium [38].
To elucidate the role of host-guest interactions for xylene separation, single-crystal structures of the $p X\mathrm{-},m X\mathrm{-}$ , and $\phantom{+}o\mathrm{X}\cdot$ - loaded, as well as the guest-free state of MAF-89 were measured. SCXRD of guest-free MAF-89 showed a unit-cell volume shrinkage of $1.3\%$ from the as-synthesized state, including $-0.12\%$ of the $a/b$ -axis and $-1.0\%$ of the $c$ -axis, which were similar with the PXRD refinement result (Figure S17 and Table S1). After the adsorption of $p\mathrm{X},m\mathrm{X}$ , or $\ o X$ , the unit-cell volume expanded $1.5\%$ , $1.2\%$ , and $1.0\%$ , respectively (Table S1). In the crystal structure, $p\mathrm{X}$ locates at one of the two ends of the 0D pore (two-fold symmetric disorder) to maximize host-guest interactions. Besides the same two-fold symmetric disorder, $m X$ and $\ o X$ further show two-fold positional disorders, giving more positional confliction probability between the guest locations, which is consistent with their lower experimental saturation adsorption amounts (Figure 3). Considering both the experimental saturation uptakes and the residual electrons in pores of the crystal structures [49], the $p\mathrm{X},m\mathrm{X}$ , and $\ o\mathrm{X}$ guest molecules in the crystal structures were constrained as $100\%$ , $87\%$ , and $75\%$ of the full occupancy, respectively (Table S5).
Density functional theory (DFT) calculations gave adsorption enthalpies of $-120.8$ , $-105.0$ , and $-97.9\mathrm{\bar{kJ}m o l\bar{\Phi}^{1}}$ for $p\mathrm{X}$ , $m X$ , and $\ o X$ , including host-guest binding energies of $-128.4$ , $-117.9/-113.9$ , and $-116.4/-102.2\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}$ and host deformation energies of 7.63, 9.48/12.3, and $10.0/12.9\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}$ , respectively (Table S5), meaning that the thermodynamic selectivity is also $p\mathrm{X}>m\mathrm{X}>o\mathrm{X}$ . Differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) measurements gave adsorption enthalpies of $-94.5$ , $-77.5$ , and $-71.3\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{\bar{mol}}^{-1}$ for $p\mathrm{X},m\mathrm{X}$ , and $o X$ , respectively, which is consistent with the DFT results (Figure 2f and Figure S18). In the simulated host-guest structures, the guest locations fitted well to the SCXRD results (Figure 3 and Figure S19). Since the quality of the SCXRD data was not very high (including degradation of crystallinity and multiple disorder), the DFT results can describe more accurate guest configurations, particularly for the hydrogen atoms. The short inter-atomic separations (shorter than or similar to the sum of corresponding van der Waals radii) between the guest H atoms and the host C/N/O atoms and between the guest C atoms and the host $\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{N}$ atoms (aromatic, $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction) indicate attractive interactions, while those between the guest $\mathrm{~H~}$ atoms and the host H atoms indicate repulsive interactions (Figure S20). Note that the guest C atoms and host O atoms are all separated above $3.4\textup{\AA}$ , meaning that their interactions can be neglected. As shown in Figure S20 and Table S7, the numbers of attractive and repulsive interactions followed $p\mathrm{X}>m\mathrm{X}>o\mathrm{X}$ and $p X<$ $m\mathrm{X}\approx o\mathrm{X}$ , respectively, meaning that the pore size/shape and surface functionality fit $p\mathrm{X}$ best and $\ o\mathrm{X}$ worst.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, by using the classical metal and ligand building blocks, a new MOF with unique coordination network topology, small pore size, and relatively large voids was successfully synthesized to simultaneously achieve ultra-high selectivity, high adsorption amount, and fast adsorption rate for $p\mathrm{X}$ separation from xylene isomers. Similar with some ultramicroporous adsorbents, the small pores can distinguish the different shapes of the xylene isomers to give the preferred thermodynamic selectivities. However, the ultrahigh selectivity is dominated by kinetic mechanism, which should be attributed to the gating actions of the flexible ultra-small apertures. In contrast, the host-deformation thermodynamic effect showed less influence toward separation, highlighting the importance of kinetically controlled flexibility (gating flexibility) compared with the thermodynamically controlled flexibility. Finally, the exceptionally fast $p\mathrm{X}$ adsorption rate in the ultra-narrow pores can be attributed to the relatively high porosity originating from the 3D connectivity, which can relax the coordination network (lower the gating energy barrier) to enhance the gating flexibility. These results demonstrated the potential of gating flexibility for solving the trade-off issue among adsorption selectivity, adsorption capacity and adsorption rate.
